What each test proves:

| Test | Property verified |
| --- | --- |
| `TestStaleLeaderAppendRejected` | Term fencing: an old-term `AppendEntries` cannot mutate term, log or commit. |
| `TestVoteIsDurableAcrossRestart` | I1: after a restart the node rejects a different candidate in the same term (no double vote). Duplicate requests are idempotent. |
| `TestJointConsensusNeedsBothMajorities` | I4: `{1,2}` (old only) cannot commit; `{1,3,4}` (both) can. |
| `TestSnapshotFencesStaleLeader` | I3: stale-term, monotonicity and rollback-behind-commit snapshots are all rejected; a valid snapshot adopts its embedded joint config. |
| `TestCrashBetweenJointCommitAndSnapshotKeepsJointConfig` | I2: restart from the log **and** from the snapshot both yield the joint config, and neither `C_old` nor `C_new` alone forms a quorum. |
| `TestDeterministicReorderingAndDuplicates` | The full 3-node replication run produces byte-identical committed logs under FIFO delivery and under reversed + duplicated delivery. |

## Why this generalises

* The active configuration is a deterministic replay of the durable log from the durable snapshot config. There is no separate `conf` state field that can desync from the log, so a crash cannot produce a torn membership view.
* All safety decisions (commit, election, snapshot acceptance) route through `Configuration.HasQuorum` and term comparison, so reordering/duplication only changes *when* a message is processed, never *whether* it is safe.
* No `time.Now()`, no goroutines and no randomness appear in the package, which is what makes the tests reproduce exactly.
* The two recovery cases in test 5 correspond to crashing before and after the snapshot record is durable; both are safe, which is the crash window the problem targets.
